Funding-Text = {INUIT (Ice Nuclei research UnIT) is a multiinstitutional project
   (FOR1525, https://www.ice-nuclei.de/, last access: 20 March 2019) funded
   by the German Research Foundation (DFG). The objective of INUIT is to
   comprehensively study the atmospheric heterogeneous ice formation with
   collaboration among various research institutions. All INUIT members and
   associated partners acknowledge Birte Hulsen und Susanne Staechelin for
   central coordination and administration of the INUIT research unit. We
   thank Shaul Lapidot, Clarite Azerraf and Melodea Ltd. for providing the
   NCC samples for this research. The AIDA team (Ottmar Mohler and Naruki
   Hiranuma) and the INKA team (Kristina Hohler and Thea Schiebel)
   acknowledge the IMK-AAF engineering and infrastructure group (Georg
   Scheurig, Rainer Buschbacher, Tomasz Chudy, Olga Dombrowski, Jens
   Nadolny, Frank Schwarz and Steffen Vogt) for their continued support
   throughout INUIT-1 and INUIT-2. The CSU-CFDC team (Kaitlyn Suski, Gregg
   P. Schill and Paul J. DeMott) acknowledges the US National Science
   Foundation (NSF) for support from grant AGS1358495. Gregg P. Schill
   additionally acknowledges support from NSF postdoctoral grant
   AGS1433517. The CNR-ISAC team acknowledges funding for their research
   from the European Union's Seventh Framework Programme FP7-ENV-2013
   project BACCHUS (grant no. 603445) and Franco Corticelli (Institute for
   Microelectronics and Microsystems IMM-CNR) for SEM observations. The
   FRIDGE group acknowledges funding by DFG in the Research Unit FOR 1525
   (INUIT) under BI 462/3-2. For the LACIS work, Heike Wex was funded
   within the INUIT subproject WE 4722/1-2. The MRI cloud simulation
   chamber experiments were partly supported by JSPS KAKENHI grant numbers
   23244095 and 17H00787. Support of the PNNL-CIC work for Gourihar
   Kulkarni was provided by the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) Office of
   Biological and Environmental Research (OBER) Atmospheric Research
   Systems Program (ASR). The Pacific Northwest National Laboratory is
   operated for DOE by Battelle Memorial Institute under contract
   DE-AC05-76RLO 1830. In addition, Ottmar Mohler, Naruki Hiranuma and
   Gourihar Kulkarni acknowledge the EMSL general use grant (proposal ID
   49077) for supporting the PNNLCIC work. The BINARY group (Carsten Budke,
   Evelyn Jantsch and Thomas Koop) acknowledges funding from DFG under the
   INUIT subproject KO 2944/2-2 and also acknowledges Sabine Robrecht, who
   designed and performed experiments that contributed to the development
   of the recommended suspension preparation protocol. The CMU-CS team
   (Michael Polen, Hassan Beydoun, and Ryan C. Sullivan) was supported by
   the National Science Foundation (grant number CHE-1554941, and a
   graduate research fellowship for Michael Polen). The NIPR-CRAFT
   experiments were partly supported by JSPS KAKENHI grant numbers 15K13570
   and 16H06020. Kimberly M. Cory and Naruki Hiranuma thank the NSF-EAPSI
   for the validation of NCC as well as microscopy characterization of our
   cellulose samples conducted in collaboration with Yutaka Tobo and Kouji
   Adachi in Japan. The Leeds group thanks the European Research Council
   (ERC, 240449 ICE; 632272 IceControl; 648661; MarineIce; 713664
   CryoProtect) and the Natural Environment Research Council (NERC,
   NE/I019057/1). The part of the research based on LINDA measurements was
   supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (SNF) through grant
   nos. 200021\_140228 and 200020\_159194. For M-AL and M-WT, Miklos
   Szakall and Amelie Mayer acknowledge support from Deutsche
   Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG) under contract SZ260/4-2 within Research
   Unit INUIT (FOR 1525). The NC State group (Hans Taylor and Markus D.
   Petters) acknowledges funding from NSF-AGS 1450690. The NC State group
   thanks Danielle Dillane, Mary Hester, Chris Rohrbach, Margaret Scott,
   Hannah Tekleab and Mark Wu for their help with collecting the raw data.
   The WISDOM team (Yinon Rudich and Naama Reicher) gratefully acknowledge
   support from the Ice Nuclei Research Unit (INUIT) of the DFG, The Helen
   Kimmel Center for Planetary Sciences and the DeBotton center for Marine
   Sciences. Naruki Hiranuma and Craig L. White-side thank the HEAF and
   Killgore research grant for the development and implementation of
   WT-CRAFT. This material is based upon work supported by the U.S.
   Department of Energy, Office of Science, Office of Biological and
   Environmental Research (DE-SC0018979). For offline mass spectrometry,
   Johannes Schneider and Hans-Christian Clemen acknowledge funding from
   DFG under the INUIT subproject SCHN 1138/2-2. Additional support (Alla
   Zelenyuk, David M. Bell, Kaitlyn Suski) was provided by the U.S.
   Department of Energy (DOE) Office of Biological and Environmental
   Research (OBER) Atmospheric Research Systems Program (ASR). Part of the
   research was performed using EMSL, a DOE Office of Science User Facility
   sponsored by the Office of Biological and Environmental Research and
   located at Pacific Northwest National Laboratory. For offline
   microscopy, Kouji Adachi thanks the Global Environment Research Fund of
   the Japanese Ministry of the Environment (2-1703) and JSPS KAKENHI
   (grant numbers JP16K16188, JP15H02811 and JP16H01772). Naruki Hiranuma
   thank Soeren Zorn and Konrad Kandler for a useful discussion regarding
   potential artifact analyses at the initial stage.},
